      excerpt: "Rebranding is expensive, disruptive, and often unnecessary. Here's how to honestly assess whether your brand needs a refresh — or whether the real problem is something else entirely.",
      content: `
        <p>Every few months a business owner comes to us and says, "We need a rebrand." Sometimes they're right. More often, what they actually need is better marketing execution, a clearer value proposition, or a new website — not a new logo. Rebranding for the wrong reasons wastes money and resets brand equity you've spent years building.</p>
        <h2>Legitimate reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>Your business has fundamentally changed.</strong> You've pivoted to a different market, expanded your service offering significantly, or merged with another company. The brand no longer describes what you actually do or who you serve. This is the clearest signal that a rebrand is warranted.</p>
        <p><strong>Your audience has changed.</strong> You started targeting small local clients and now you're pitching enterprise contracts. Your brand aesthetic and voice need to match where you're going, not where you've been.</p>
        <p><strong>Your brand is genuinely creating friction.</strong> Prospects regularly misunderstand your positioning. Your name is being confused with a competitor. You're in a new market where your brand carries no meaning. These are real, measurable problems that a rebrand can solve.</p>
        <h2>Bad reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>You're bored with it.</strong> You've looked at your logo 10,000 times and you're tired of it. Your customers haven't. Brand fatigue is an internal problem — don't solve it at the expense of the recognition you've built externally.</p>
        <p><strong>Sales are down.</strong> A rebrand is not a sales strategy. If revenue is declining, the causes are almost always in your offer, pricing, sales process, or marketing channels — not your logo. Fix the real problem first.</p>
        <p><strong>A competitor rebranded.</strong> Their reasons and yours are different. Don't let a competitor's decision push you into an expensive exercise that doesn't serve your business.</p>
        <h2>The middle path: a brand refresh</h2>
        <p>Most businesses don't need a full rebrand — they need a refresh. Updating the typography, tightening the colour palette, modernising the logo mark without abandoning it, and clarifying the brand voice. This preserves the equity you have while bringing the brand up to date. It's faster, cheaper, and usually more strategically sound than starting from zero.</p>
        <h2>Before you decide, test the hypothesis</h2>
        <p>Ask ten customers — not colleagues, not family — what words come to mind when they think of your brand. Ask what they think you do. Ask if they'd recommend you. Their answers will tell you whether a brand problem actually exists. If it doesn't, spend your rebrand budget on marketing instead.</p>
