      slug: 'landing-page-conversion-design',
      title: "The Anatomy of a High-Converting Landing Page",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '18 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '7 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1581291518857-4d27a540d9f2?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most landing pages fail at the same predictable points. Here's the structure that works — backed by what we've seen across dozens of client campaigns.",
      content: `
        <p>A landing page has one job: convert a specific visitor to a specific action. Not to explain everything you do. Not to showcase your full portfolio. One page, one goal, one conversion. The pages that fail almost always do so because they try to do too much.</p>
        <h2>Above the fold: the only section that matters first</h2>
        <p>Before your visitor scrolls — often within 3 seconds — they've made a judgment. The above-the-fold section must answer three questions immediately: What is this? Who is it for? Why should I care? Your headline answers what and for whom. Your subheadline answers why. Your primary CTA button answers what to do next.</p>
        <p>The most common mistake here is leading with your company name and tagline. Nobody cares yet. Lead with the outcome the visitor gets, not who you are. "Get a website that converts visitors into customers" outperforms "Welcome to Fazed Digital" every single time.</p>
        <h2>Social proof placed before the ask</h2>
        <p>Trust must come before commitment. A visitor who doesn't trust you won't convert, no matter how compelling your offer. Place testimonials, client logos, case study callouts, or review ratings early — ideally in the first scroll, before your primary CTA. Specific social proof outperforms generic: "We increased conversions by 43% in 60 days" beats "Great to work with!" by a significant margin.</p>
        <h2>The offer section: clarity over creativity</h2>
        <p>Explain what the visitor gets, in plain language, in three to five bullet points. Use the visitor's language, not yours — describe outcomes, not features. "A website live in 4 weeks" is clearer than "Rapid deployment methodology." Avoid jargon. Every word that requires explanation is a word that loses people.</p>
        <h2>Objection handling built into the page</h2>
        <p>Every visitor has silent objections: Is this too expensive? Will it actually work for my industry? What if I'm not happy? Address these directly in an FAQ section or woven into your copy. Naming the objection and answering it honestly converts better than pretending it doesn't exist. Transparency builds the trust that gets people to click.</p>
        <h2>One CTA, repeated strategically</h2>
        <p>Your call to action should appear three times on most landing pages: above the fold, after the proof section, and at the bottom. It should say the same thing every time — not "Learn More" in one place and "Get Started" in another. Consistency reduces decision fatigue. Make the button text describe the action specifically: "Book a Free Strategy Call" is stronger than "Submit."</p>
        <h2>Speed and mobile — the invisible conversion killers</h2>
        <p>A landing page that takes more than 3 seconds to load on a mobile device loses a significant portion of its visitors before they've read a word. Mobile now accounts for over 60% of web traffic. If your landing page isn't designed and tested on mobile first, you're optimising for the minority. Run every page through Google's PageSpeed Insights before you run any paid traffic to it.</p>
      `,
